 3826: ## Round #500 pass 5 -- the disposition I had ACCEPTED was wrong, and a seat overturned it by reading
 3827: 
 3828: `80cab9e` shipped a 35-row detector and closed with a disclosure: a mask narrowing at bits
 3829: 24-25 collapses the two IMPLEMENTED primitives onto each other, and I recorded that as
 3830: **uncoverable** -- no host kill restored, and no pixel oracle at reset.  `M_NARROW_25` was
 3831: built anyway and its escape PREDICTED IN WRITING, three tables running.
 3832: 
 3833: **Pass 5 refused the round.  Four of five answering seats said the chain does not close.**
 3834: 
 3835: ### The finding, and how it was found
 3836: 
 3837: `case DE_PRIM_LINE:` opens with a diagnostic present in **that arm and nowhere else**:
 3838: 
 3839: ```c
 3840: 	if (drawmode & DE_DRAWMODE_XFER_EN)
 3841: 		fatal("[ sgi_de: XFER_EN for LINE op? ]\n");
 3842: ```
 3843: 
 3844: So the two primitives ARE distinguishable, with no pixel oracle at all: set `XFER_EN`, draw
 3845: a RECTANGLE, and the message must not appear.  Under `& 0xfd000000` the rectangle folds onto
 3846: LINE and it does.
 3847: 
 3848: > **EVERY FACT IN MY DISCLOSURE WAS TRUE AND THE CONCLUSION DID NOT FOLLOW.**  A collapse
 3849: > really does restore no host kill; a pixel oracle really does not exist at reset.  But *"no
 3850: > pixel oracle"* is not *"no oracle"* -- and **I had grepped that `XFER_EN` line myself
 3851: > earlier in the same round without connecting it.**
 3852: 
 3853: *Found by the grok seat, which I had asked to be HARDER on its own earlier "the chain of this
 3854: class ends here" call.  It reversed itself and then found the hole in mine:* **"I was wrong
 3855: then; I am not going to make the same call on a leftover I have not finished enumerating."**
 3856: 
 3857: ### The repair: THREE rows, because one would have been vacuous
 3858: 
 3859: | row | asserts |
 3860: |---|---|
 3861: | `n25 rect` | `count("XFER_EN for LINE op?") == 0` -- RECTANGLE must not reach the LINE-only arm |
 3862: | `n25 rectliv` | the guest runs on -- so the row above is not satisfied by nothing happening |
 3863: | `n25 line` | `count(...) == 1` -- the SAME channel DOES fire for LINE |
 3864: 
 3865: **`n25 rect` alone is negative-only**: satisfied if the channel is dead for any unrelated
 3866: reason -- a wrong register offset, a guard misread, a drawmode that never lands.  `n25 line`
 3867: proves it is live.  *That is this round's own `xlatch all` lesson applied three hours late,
 3868: and this time the companion went in BEFORE the table rather than after a nine-mutant sweep
 3869: found the hole.*
 3870: 
 3871: ### What the pair does NOT catch -- named, not left unknown
 3872: 
 3873: The glm seat, asked for the smallest edit defeating the pair, found one: **a single added
 3874: conjunct**, `(drawmode & XFER_EN) && !(op & 0x02000000)`, silences the diagnostic for a
 3875: folded RECTANGLE while keeping it firing for a genuine LINE.  Both rows stay green.  It
 3876: reached that by elimination and said why nothing smaller works -- the two rows differ in
 3877: exactly ONE input, so any edit leaving both green must consult `op` itself.
 3878: 
 3879: **DISCLOSED, not repaired, on a SCOPE rule rather than difficulty:** that guard is product
 3880: code this round does not touch, and clause (d)'s enumeration is closed over the diff's own
 3881: hunks.  A conjunct on an unrelated guard is coverage debt to state, not a floor breach to
 3882: repair.  Also recorded: `n25 line` depends on there being exactly ONE emission site for that
 3883: string (`grep -c` = 1 today) -- **a layout fact, not an invariant.**
 3884: 
 3885: ### Table 4 -- 22 mutants, 22 predictions, zero surprises, and NO clean sweep but BASE
 3886: 
 3887: Detector **35 -> 38** rows; gate repinned and verified against the probe's own emission.
 3888: Gate 14 ARM: **PASS 549 checks, 0 FAIL**, primop 38 emitted / 38 green / 0 red.
 3889: Logs: `regress/artifacts/sgideprimop/killtable_20260902_table4/`.
 3890: 
