tools/pipeline/ledger.json:24282:   "note": "*** MY OWN BRIEF'S PREMISE WAS THAT `gdromcondlies` HELD THE LAST LIVE GUEST-REACHABLE HOST KILLS.  IT IS FALSE, AND THE FIRST FILE I SAMPLED REFUTED IT. ***  I stated it AS A QUESTION in that brief (*'have I confused \"the queue knows of no others\" with \"there are no others\"?'*), which is the only reason this was cheap to find -- and I found it myself, before the panel answered.  **THE CENSUS SAID WHERE TO LOOK AND WAS RIGHT.**  `exit_census.py` reports 99 live terminating calls inside `DEVICE_ACCESS` across 30 files; `dev_sgi_re.c` carries **21**, seven times the gdrom file.  It is the top of that list, and I sampled it for exactly that reason.  *** WHAT IS THERE, AND IT IS A CLASS THIS PROJECT HAS ALREADY FIXED ELSEWHERE: ***  the guard at the head of the CRIME_RE_TLB_A arm is  `if (len != 8) { fatal(\"TODO: unimplemented len=%i ...\"); exit(1); }`  -- **a GUEST CHOOSING AN ACCESS WIDTH ends the host process.**  That is the `#337` class verbatim, whose own comment in `dev_wdc.c` reads *'a 3- or 8-byte access to the data port used to call exit(1) -- a GUEST choosing an access width killed the host process'*.  The adjacent arm kills on a plain READ (`fatal(\"TODO: read from CRIME_RE_TLB_A\"); exit(1);`), which is the direction/offset class.  **REACHABILITY CHECKED, NOT ASSUMED**: `machine_sgi.c:500` calls `dev_sgi_re_init(machine, mem, 0x15000000)` and the device registers at `baseaddr + 0x1000` through `memory_device_register`.  It is a real device on a real machine, mapped into the guest address space.  **HONEST BOUND ON THE NUMBER, because this project keeps getting counts wrong: 21 is how many live terminating calls sit inside that file's `DEVICE_ACCESS` handlers.**  It is NOT a claim that 21 are individually reachable -- some may be guarded, and `dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:1055` is the standing proof that a `DEVICE_ACCESS` site can be unreachable and take a hand-derived warrant to establish it.  What IS established: at least two of them are reachable by construction, and they are of classes already recognised as defects here.  *** THE GENERAL LESSON, AND IT IS ABOUT THE QUEUE RATHER THAN THIS DEVICE: A QUEUE-COMPLETE CLAIM IS NOT A TREE-COMPLETE ONE. ***  The adjudicating seat flagged exactly this limit about its own answer hours earlier -- *'my no-other-live-host-kills claim rests on the queue and this file's grep, not a fresh tree-wide exit census'* -- and then I wrote the queue-complete version into a brief as a premise anyway.  The seat named the gap; the brief inherited it.  SCOPE: this is a CLUSTER and must not become one round.  The round-scope rule takes ONE site, with a second joining only if it is witnessed, in the same device and semantic class, and covered by the same detector with no new oracle.  The width guards may qualify as one class; the read-direction kills are a second.  **More witnesses create more candidate ROUNDS, never bigger rounds.**  NEXT STEP is a rung-3 witness on ONE site, not a fix: `-E sgi` machines exist in tree, the device is mapped at a known base, and the `#337` precedent gives the fix shape (latched decline, `(once)` marker) if a witness lands.  *** THE DIAGNOSIS ABOVE IS INCOMPLETE AND THE TRUE ONE IS WORSE.  Corrected the same day by the opus5 seat, which MEASURED it. ***  This row was filed saying the brief had confused 'the queue knows of no others' with 'there are no others'.  **Neither.  The queue knew of NINE others.**  Ledger row `n6` is OPEN, titled *'The record says the footbridge host-halts were made graceful; nine of them are not'*, carrying a CONFIRMED rung-3 witness (`probe:regress/footbridge_sites_probe.py` row K1, gated in `gate_arm.sh`) and a `state_note` RANKING IT 6th OF 8 on 2026-08-27 -- three days before the brief claimed there were none.  The seat rebuilt HEAD clean and re-ran the COMMITTED probe: a guest `ldr r7,[r0,#392]` on an unmodified `-E cats` ends the host at `dev_footbridge.c:589`.  It then swapped the single planted word three more times and got **four of four** -- `:604` ENABLE_CLEAR read, `:565` IRQ_STATUS write, `:712` SDRAM_BA_MASK read -- each one guest instruction, each with the site's own diagnostic text as the attribution control.  **So this is the UNREAD-ROW class, not a census gap.**  The census work still stands and still found two REAL clusters nobody had filed; what it did not do is what a `grep n6` would have done in one second.  *The lesson is not 'census harder'.  It is that a completeness claim must be checked against THE QUEUE'S OWN OPEN ROWS before it is written down -- and that is the cheaper check of the two.*  *** THE CENSUS IS DONE, AND IT SPLITS THE 21 INTO FOUR CLASSES WITH ONE PROVABLY DEAD SITE.  agy seat, 2026-08-30, `regress/artifacts/widthkillclass/witness_20260830/`. ***  It is a READING, not a measurement, and is recorded as one -- but the single most surprising claim in it was CHECKED HERE BY OPENING THE FILE, and it held.  **20 of 21 guest-reachable; 1 PROVABLY UNREACHABLE.**  WIDTH (a guest choosing an access size ends the host) -- 7 reachable: the `len != 8` guards on CRIME_RE_TLB_A/B/C, CRIME_RE_TEX and CRIME_RE_LINEAR_A/B, plus `sgi_de: len = %i not implemented` (which wants len 4 or 8).  DIRECTION (a legal-width access in the wrong direction) -- 6 reachable: `TODO: read from CRIME_RE_TLB_A/B/C`, `..._TEX`, `..._LINEAR_A/B`, each reached by an 8-byte READ, i.e. **the guest does everything right except the direction**.  BADOFFSET -- 1: the `unimplemented read/write to address` arm, reached by any access to an unregistered sub-window.  BADVAL -- 6: `sgi_de: UNIMPLEMENTED drawing op`, and five `sgi_mte` arms (MTE_MODE_STIPPLE, unimplemented SRC, MTE_MODE_COPY, MTE_DEPTH_x, and an unimplemented bytemask).  *** THE UNREACHABLE ONE IS DEAD CODE AND MUST NOT BE 'FIXED' -- I VERIFIED THIS BY OPENING THE FILE RATHER THAN ACCEPTING IT. ***  `[ sgi_mte: UNIMPLEMENTED read/write len %i ]` sits in the trailing `else` of `if (len == 4) { ... } else if (len != 4) { ... } else { fatal; exit(1); }`.  For every integer `len` one of the first two arms is taken, so the third can never execute.  A round that 'de-fatalises' it would be editing unreachable code and its detector could carry no row -- **the vacuous shape in its purest form, and the census is what stops it being attempted.**  **WHAT THIS MEANS FOR SCOPE, and it is the useful part: FOUR classes means at least four candidate rounds, not one.**  The round-scope rule takes ONE site, with a second joining only if it is the same device AND the same semantic class AND covered by the same detector with no new oracle.  A WIDTH guard and a DIRECTION guard sit in the SAME ARM at the same offset and are still different classes -- whether they nonetheless share one fix design is an open question this row does not settle, and it is exactly what the next research pass should answer rather than assume.  HONEST BOUND: reachability here is derived from address-decode reading, NOT measured, except for the WIDTH class -- which now has a rung-3 witness (see `widthkillclass`).  The DIRECTION, BADOFFSET and BADVAL classes have NO witness yet and a round on any of them owes one first.  *** THE COUNT'S SCOPE WAS WRONG AND A SEAT CHECKED IT: 21 IS THE FILE TOTAL, NOT THE HANDLER'S. ***  `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_re)` holds **13**.  Per handler: sgi_re 13, sgi_de 1, sgi_mte 1, sgi_de_status 0, helpers 6 -- summing to the 21 this row filed.  So the four-class census spans FOUR HANDLERS, not one, and that matters for scope rather than for tidiness: a round joining sites 'in the same handler' is a different population from one joining sites 'in the same file'.  **This is the dropped-qualifier class again** -- the number was right and the noun was wrong, which is exactly how `b120r`'s NIL and `#464`'s 'floods identically' went wrong.  AND THE WIDTH FIGURE OF 7 IS SHORT BY ONE against the message text: there are EIGHT width-shaped kills in the file, the six in sgi_re plus `sgi_de: len = %i not implemented` and `[ sgi_mte: UNIMPLEMENTED read/write len %i ]`.  Both are now filed separately -- `sgidewidth` (LIVE, high) and `sgimtewidth` (a mis-service plus a resurrection trap) -- because they sit in different handlers and so cannot join a sgi_re round.  *** READ-ONLY AUDIT, agy seat, 2026-08-30 -- AND THE HEADLINE IS THAT THIS ROUND MAY BE MUCH SMALLER THAN IT LOOKS: ALL SIX ARMS LOOK LIKE **ACCEPT**, NOT DECLINE. ***  The de-fatalisation option set has four members, and the fourth applies where the model already holds the right answer and merely discards it.  **`re_tlb_a`, `re_tlb_b`, `re_tlb_c`, `re_tex`, `re_linear_a` and `re_linear_b` are all WRITTEN by the store path**, so a read can return what was written -- no latch, no message, because there is no event to announce.  *A bounded-announce here would ship a wrong record in product code: printing 'unimplemented; ignored' about a read that is implemented and is not ignored.*  **REACHABILITY: all six are reachable by an ordinary guest load on unmodified `-E sgi -e ip32`**, and the addresses are derived rather than guessed -- `machine_sgi.c:500` passes base `0x15000000`, `dev_sgi_re_init` registers `sgi_re` at `baseaddr + 0x1000` for `DEV_SGI_RE_LENGTH` (0x1000), and the handler restores the full offset with `relative_addr += 0x1000`.  **Every window lies inside `0x15001000-0x150017ff`, none is shadowed, and the `else if` cascade is strictly disjoint.**  Verified here independently by opening `machine_sgi.c` and `crmfbreg.h`: the six constants are `0x1000 / 0x1200 / 0x1400 / 0x1600 / 0x1700 / 0x1780`.  **GUEST KSEG1 ADDRESSES, so a probe author does not re-derive them:** TLB_A `0xb5001000`, TLB_B `0xb5001200`, TLB_C `0xb5001400`, TEX `0xb5001600`, LINEAR_A `0xb5001700`, LINEAR_B `0xb5001780` -- read kills at lines 245, 262, 279, 296, 346, 362 respectively.  **One malloc serves all four handlers** (`dev_sgi_re_init` allocates one `struct sgi_re_data` and hands it to `sgi_de`, `sgi_mte` and `sgi_de_status`), so whatever latch infrastructure `#497` lands is already reachable here.  *The audit and the brief that produced it are preserved at `regress/artifacts/sgirekills/audit_20260830/` -- the ledger first cited the `_scratchpad/` copy, which the durable-path rule excludes precisely because it evaporates.  This row now has everything a rung-3 witness needs except the witness itself, which is the point of asking a seat for the addresses rather than the answer.*  *** THIS ROW'S TITLE SAID '21 TERMINATING CALLS INSIDE DEVICE_ACCESS' AND THAT WAS THE SAME CONFLATION I MADE AND CORRECTED ELSEWHERE TODAY.  Corrected 2026-08-30. ***  **21 is the FILE total.  15 are inside a `DEVICE_ACCESS`, and 13 of those in `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_re)`** -- the other 6 live in `draw_primitive` (1) and `do_mte_transfer` (5), neither of which is a `DEVICE_ACCESS` and neither of which a guest reaches by an ordinary access.  *** AND THE TITLE WAS NOT CARELESS -- IT FAITHFULLY REPORTED WHAT THE TOOL SAID, WHICH WAS WRONG. ***  See `exitcensus`: `exit_census.py`'s per-file totals were correct all along, but its BY-ENCLOSING-CONSTRUCT split attributed every call in a plain non-static function to the preceding `DEVICE_ACCESS`.  That bucket is a REACHABILITY claim used for ranking -- *on a guest access path BY CONSTRUCTION* -- so the defect over-stated how many host kills a guest can actually reach.  Fixed; tree-wide the bucket falls **97 -> 83**.  *** THE ACCEPT DESIGN, ADJUDICATED 2026-08-30 -- AND IT REFUTES THE AUDIT RECORDED DIRECTLY ABOVE IT ON ONE ARM OF SIX. ***  **agy ruled ACCEPT for all six read arms on the ground that BACKING STATE EXISTS in the struct.  That is not the ACCEPT test.**  The doctrine's fourth option-set member -- minted from the footbridge IRQEnable precedent -- is *the model already computes the CORRECT answer and merely discards it*.  Existence of state is necessary and nowhere near sufficient.  **FIVE ARMS ARE CLEAN ACCEPT; TEX IS NOT.**  **TLB A/B/C:** the store packs big-endian halfword pairs from raw bus bytes and a readback through the same index expression round-trips BYTE-EXACTLY on this machine -- with a premise that belongs in the comment rather than assumed: `machine_sgi.c` pins BIG ENDIAN, and while the STORE path is byte-order-agnostic the READ path is not.  **LINEAR A/B:** the store splits `idata` into two uint32 cells and the readback is its exact inverse through the `readmax64`/`writemax64` pair -- byte-order symmetric BY CONSTRUCTION, so it needs no endianness premise at all.  *** TEX FAILS THE TEST, AND THE EVIDENCE IS A GREP: `re_tex` HAS ZERO CONSUMERS ANYWHERE. ***  `tlb_a/b/c` feed `horrible_getputpixel`; `linear_a/b` feed it and `do_mte_transfer`; **`re_tex` feeds nothing.**  Its state is written through the `>> 3` index that `sgitexindex` records as a defect -- frames overlap by three, 81 of 112 cells unreachable -- so **the model does not hold the right answer for TEX; it holds a self-consistent WRONG one.**  ACCEPT is available only in the MIRRORED form (the read uses the same `>> 3` expression), which round-trips per frame while shipping hardware-wrong reads at overlapping offsets.  **That is a materially weaker claim than the other five arms', and the round record must not blur them.**  **WHAT ACCEPT OWES THAT DECLINE DOES NOT -- five items, and the second is an ORDERING DEPENDENCY on `#497`:**  (1) **round-trip rows with DISTINCT sentinels per window** -- distinctness catches a cross-wired read, while a same-window index error breaks round-trip because the store frame is position-dependent.  The JOINT translation (store and read shifted together) escapes round-trip BY CONSTRUCTION -- clause (f) territory -- so the contract names it outside, and names honestly that an out-of-decode anchor channel exists for four arms (the rendering and MTE paths consume them) and **does not exist for `re_tex` today**.  (2) **a DEVICE-SIGNATURE control**, because a faithful round-trip through a device is value-indistinguishable from RAM -- the witness ladder's own two-control lesson.  The clean discriminator: **post-`#497` a 4-byte read DECLINES and returns 0 while RAM would serve it.**  So `#497` is an ordering dependency for this round's WITNESS QUALITY, not merely for its latch infrastructure.  (3) **the dead tail's first execution belongs to `#497`, not here.**  A width-declined READ falls through the splice and runs `memory_writemax64` with `odata = 0`.  This round's new surface is narrower than it looks: **NONZERO `odata` through the BE packing**, not 'the tail runs'.  (4) **the claim must be SCOPED**: *returns what the store path recorded*, never *faithful hardware readback*.  TEX refutes the stronger phrase today, and the LINEAR arms store the PROM's own wrongly-sign-extended values verbatim -- returning them faithfully is right; modelling hardware readback is unproven.  **That is `b716a34`'s lesson pre-applied: do not put the strong phrase in a site comment.**  (5) **a PRE-WRITE read row** returning 0, which pins the memset-init as guest-visible ABI and is the minimal de-fatalisation survival row.  *** TEX POISONS THE WARRANT, NOT THE ROUND -- AND THE COUPLING IS ENFORCEABLE BY ONE ROW RATHER THAN BY A DEPENDENCY. ***  Ship TEX MIRRORED plus its round-trip row, and any ONE-SIDED future fix reds that row loudly: store `>> 1` against read `>> 3` writes cells 4-7 and reads 1-4, and the reverse likewise, while the JOINT fix keeps identity and the row stays green.  **So the round-trip row IS the coupling enforcement**, and `sgitexindex`'s fix becomes a declared two-site edit with a tripwire already in place.  Recorded in BOTH rows.  **WHAT IS NOT ACCEPTABLE:** unmirrored `>> 1` reads over the `>> 3` store -- round-trip broken immediately, silent wrong data, worse than a decline -- or ACCEPT recorded with the same warrant strength as the other five arms.  **A LATENT THIS ROUND INHERITS RATHER THAN CREATES:** the TLB arms mask the INITIAL `tlbi` but increment it UNMASKED in the loop, so `relative_addr & 0x1ff >= 0x1fa` with `len 8` runs to index 0x102 -- three halfwords into the adjacent array.  Guest-unreachable for the alignment reason `sgilinearoob` settles path-by-path, and **a mirrored read arm makes it a second SITE of that class** -- record it there so an eventual bound fixes both directions.  *** THE TEST THAT KILLED `sgideoob` WAS THEN APPLIED TO THE SURVIVORS, BY ENUMERATION RATHER THAN BY REASONING -- AND BOTH HOLD.  2026-08-30. ***  Withdrawing a false member is only half the follow-through; the other half is asking whether the same miss is in the rows that remain.  The refuting method for `sgideoob` was *what is this index's ACTUAL RANGE?* -- so every reachable `relative_addr` in each window was walked and the resulting index recorded.  **TLB LOOP** (`re_tlb_a[256]`, `tlbi = ((ra & 0x1ff) >> 1) & 0xff`, four writes at `len 8`): with 8-byte ALIGNMENT -- the only thing a guest can produce -- the maximum index written is **255, IN BOUNDS**.  Unaligned, reachable only by the debugger's `put d`, it is **258, OOB by three halfwords**.  *Exactly as recorded: guest-unreachable, debugger-reachable, same disposition as `sgilinearoob`.*  **`sgitexindex`** (`re_tex[112]`, `tlbi = ((ra & 0xff) >> 3) & 0xff`, window `0x1600-0x16df`): maximum index written is **30, comfortably IN BOUNDS of [112]** -- so this row is NOT an out-of-bounds and never claimed to be.  Its three stated figures were each re-derived independently and all match: **81 cells unreachable**, **3 entries of overlap** between consecutive 8-byte writes.  *** SO THE CLASS AFTER THE WITHDRAWAL IS: TWO REAL OOBs (`sgilinearoob`, the TLB loop), BOTH GUEST-SHIELDED BY ALIGNMENT AND BOTH DEBUGGER-REACHABLE; ONE NON-OOB CORRECTNESS DEFECT (`sgitexindex`); AND ONE WITHDRAWN FALSE MEMBER. ***  Every one now tested by the same method, which is what the false member's whole lesson was.  *** THE WITNESS FIELD WAS STALE AND SAID THE OPPOSITE OF WHAT SHIPPED.  Corrected 2026-08-30 by the pass-2 seat. ***  It read *no rung-3 probe has been built and the round owes one before any edit* -- **false since `#497` shipped six `ld kill` rows**, each a real guest `ld` at one of the six windows, dying with per-site attribution text, on an unmodified machine.  The row's note mentioned them nowhere.  *** AND THE SAME ROWS CHANGE THIS ROW'S COST IN THE OTHER DIRECTION, WHICH MUST BE WRITTEN DOWN NOW RATHER THAN REDISCOVERED: FIXING THIS DEFECT WILL TURN A GREEN GATE RED BY SUCCEEDING. ***  Six rows' worth.  That is precisely the `n6` shape adjudicated hours earlier in this same session, so this round inherits its ordering obligation: **re-pin the six rows FIRST, measure the committed build RED under the new expectations, and only then edit** -- same scripts, opposite colour.  Without that order the re-pin is observationally identical to fitting the test to the binary.  *** THE HEADLINE COUNT INCLUDES ONE SITE THAT CAN NEVER EXECUTE.  Derived 2026-08-30. ***  `dev_sgi_re.c` holds 20 raw `exit(1)` lines; exactly FIVE are commented out (179, 188, 280, 630, 1122) and fifteen are live -- **including 1334, which is inside `sgi_mte`'s unreachable `else` (see `sgimtewidth`).**  **`exit_census.py` excludes COMMENTED-OUT code and cannot see REACHABILITY.**  That is correct behaviour for a text-level tool and it is a limit worth stating rather than discovering: \"live terminating calls\" means SYNTACTICALLY live.  The row's numbers are not wrong -- they are the census's numbers -- but a round quoting them as reachable host kills would be over-claiming by one.  *** THE `sgirekills` / `sgitexindex` COUPLING, ESTABLISHED 2026-08-31 AND RECORDED ON BOTH ROWS SO NEITHER CAN BE CLOSED IN IGNORANCE OF THE OTHER. ***  **The fact, which is settled:** `sgitexindex`'s `>> 1` is correct under BOTH candidate readings of a TEX entry -- 112 halfwords indexed `offset >> 1`, or 28 64-bit entries where entry `k` starts at halfword `4k`, since `(offset >> 3) * 4 == offset >> 1` for 8-aligned offsets.  **So the store's shift is wrong INDEPENDENTLY of the missing datasheet**, and there is no SGI document in `_scratchpad/`.  **Consequence for `sgirekills`:** its TEX read arm can only ACCEPT in the MIRRORED form -- reusing the store's `>> 3` -- because only that round-trips what the store wrote.  Mirroring therefore enshrines a KNOWN-WRONG index in a second place, and gives it a green round-trip row.  *When someone later corrects the shift, that row goes RED because of their correct fix.*  **Consequence for `sgitexindex`, in the other direction:** the TEX read arm is TODAY a live host kill, so **no guest can read back what those writes stored** and the aliasing is currently unobservable from inside the guest.  Fixing `sgirekills` is what would expose it.  That bears on severity and on ORDER, and neither row had recorded it.  *Neither consequence decides the disposition -- that is the question `sgirekills` is held for and a panel is answering it.  What is recorded here is the COUPLING, which is a fact either way.*  *** THE HELD TEX-READ QUESTION IS ANSWERED: DO NOT MIRROR.  3 of 3 packet-fed seats, and the dissenter's FALLBACK is the other two's first choice, so the convergence is total. ***  deepseek and minimax: **decline + latched announce** until the index fix lands.  glm: **BACKED ACCEPT with the CORRECT `>> 1` index**, round-trip oracle pinned to correct semantics and honestly red until `sgitexindex` lands -- *with decline + latched announce as its explicit fallback, and mirrored ACCEPT ranked worst of the three by all three seats.*  **MY PREMISE WAS CIRCULAR AND A SEAT SAID SO USING MY OWN INLINED FINDING.**  See its entry above.  The practical upshot: the choice is between *decline* and *correct-index read*, and MIRRORING -- the option the row was held on -- is off the table.  *One caution against glm's first choice, which it half-concedes: a correct-index read against a wrong-index store means a guest that writes then reads gets data from a different place, which is arguably worse for the guest than a decline returning 0.  That is why all three seats land on decline as at least an acceptable answer.*  **AND ONE OBJECTION OF ITS OWN IS ANSWERED BY A GUARD I FAILED TO INLINE.**  glm marked the *31 of 112 / overlap by three* arithmetic NOT SETTLEABLE because it presupposes 8-byte-wide 8-byte-aligned stores, asserted nowhere in the packet.  Correct as a packet criticism: `#497`'s width guard on this very arm declines `len != 8`, so the width is established, and 8-byte ALIGNMENT follows from the MIPS path (`ld`/`sd` raise ADEL/ADES before `memory_rw`, and the unaligned helper masks the address itself).  **A packet defect of mine, not an analysis defect of its.**",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:24293:     "outcome_note": "[UPHELD-ON-FIVE-ARMS-REFUTED-ON-THE-SIXTH]  Its census confirmation and address derivation are CHECKED and correct: I verified the base registration in `machine_sgi.c`, the `+0x1000` offset in `dev_sgi_re_init`, and all six window constants in `crmfbreg.h`.  **But its ACCEPT ruling used the wrong test.**  It ruled all six read arms ACCEPT because BACKING STATE EXISTS; the doctrine's test is that the model already computes the CORRECT answer.  Refuted on TEX by the adjudicating seat and re-verified here by grep: `re_tex` appears three times in the whole file -- declaration, store, and a debug printing back what was just stored.  ZERO consumers, against 6 references for re_linear_a and 4 for re_tlb_a.  *Graded partial rather than UPHELD because five of six is the honest figure, and a blanket UPHELD would have shipped TEX's ACCEPT with the same warrant strength as the clean arms.*",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:24302:     "ev": "_scratchpad/ (design adjudication; re_tex consumer grep re-checkable in-tree)",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:24303:     "note": "Refuted the agy audit on ONE arm of six and upheld it on five -- the useful shape, because a blanket confirmation would have shipped TEX's ACCEPT with the same warrant strength as the clean arms.  Its discriminator is the doctrine's own wording ('the model already computes the CORRECT answer') against the audit's paraphrase ('backing state exists'), and it settled TEX by GREPPING FOR CONSUMERS rather than by arguing: re_tex feeds nothing anywhere in the file.  Also supplied the five ACCEPT obligations, the #497 ordering dependency for witness quality, and the round-trip row as coupling enforcement for sgitexindex.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:24305:     "outcome_note": "[UPHELD-AND-IT-CAUGHT-A-DESIGN-ERROR-I-HAD-ACCEPTED]  *** SELF-GRADING DISCLOSED: the main loop and this seat are the same family, so this rate should not be read as accuracy. ***  Every load-bearing claim was CHECKED independently before recording: the `re_tex` consumer grep (three references, all declaration/store/debug), and the `sgi_de` tail side-effect -- `if (startFlag) draw_primitive(cpu, d);` runs BEFORE the read writeback, and `startFlag` is computed for ANY access, so a naive fall-through would draw over stale state on a malformed one.  **That last finding is why the grade is worth recording: the OBVIOUS fix, copied from `#497` by analogy, would have shipped a new defect the old kill was preventing.**",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:24327:     "note": "*** REFUTED THE BRIEF'S CENTRAL PREMISE AS CIRCULAR, using the finding the brief itself inlined. ***  I wrote that mirrored is the ONLY ACCEPT form.  That holds only if ACCEPT is STIPULATED to mean *round-trip the current `>> 3` store*.  Since `>> 1` is correct under both entry-size readings, **an ACCEPT in CORRECT form is constructible today**; what is impossible is only a read that is SIMULTANEOUSLY hardware-right and round-trip-faithful to the unfixed store.  *The premise ruled out the third option by definition -- the question was begged, and the six-member set even names the member (BACKED ACCEPT).*  **And its P2 destroys mirroring's advertised benefit**: I called the mirrored readback *self-consistent but hardware-wrong at overlapping offsets*, and BOTH halves are misstated. Wrongness is not confined to overlaps -- mirrored index `m` diverges from correct `4m` at EVERY offset >= 8.  And self-consistency holds only in single-store isolation: with the overlap-by-three the brief itself states, a store at +8 lands in `re_tex[1..4]` and one at +16 in `[2..5]`, so a mirrored read at +8 returns a MIX of two stores -- not *what the store actually wrote*.  **Mirroring's benefit exists only for a guest that writes one slot and reads it back before touching another.**"
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:24363:     "note": "**RETROSPECTIVE BATCH 6 (dev_sgi_re.c) -- THE SEAT THAT REFUSED THIS PACKET THREE TIMES ANSWERED IT.**  The batch had been written off and covered by a Grok substitute; once the consequence vocabulary was neutralised, GPT-6 Astra at max effort returned 474 KB (answer at _scratchpad/retro_batch6_sgire/ANSWER_astra_20260906.txt).  Recovering a seat three refusals had lost is the process result; what it found is below.  **A NAMED DETECTOR ESCAPE, predicted with its score**: sgi_de_primop_probe.py's rows `rd surv' and `rd decl' never consume the printed read result and never require continued execution, so terminating with status ZERO after drawing inside the START branch -- only for reads -- preserves both.  Predicted **51/51**, a total escape.  It checked the other sessions and none supplies that START-read continuation check.  **And it declined to file it twice**: the class is already `sgideprimrdpair' in the records, so it belongs there rather than as a new row -- the discipline this project asks for and rarely gets from a seat with a finding in hand.  **TWO STALE CONTRACT CLAIMS in that probe, both named by anchor**: the sentence at `Every survival row is' remains FALSE, and the claim that only the `two once' row catches loss of the latch setting is STALE, because later repeated-draw rows catch it too.  **A COVERAGE CENSUS I CAN CHECK**: all four sgi probes are wired through gate_arm.sh at the anchors `python3 sgi_re_width_probe.py', `sgi_de_width_probe.py', `sgi_re_unmapped_probe.py' and `sgi_de_primop_probe.py'; its static enumeration gives 35, 30, 50 and 51 rows, and every emitted name matches its gate list -- the padded-column / unlisted-row class checked across four probes at once.  **A MISSING-CONTROL FINDING that is a class rather than an instance**: it verified `#497''s preserved NULL-MEDIUM receipt and could NOT locate equivalent receipts in the `#498', `#499' or `#500' artifact sets.  A null-medium control is vacuity-floor clause (c); if those rounds ran one and did not preserve it, the receipts are owed, and if they did not run one, the clause was unmet.  Either way it is checkable and nobody has checked it.  It re-confirms the historical tables as honest (19/19 baseline, 5/19 pre-fix, 14/19 for the zero-status termination variant, 13/19 for suppressed announcement, 18/19 for each latch/bit-alias variant; the L2 table 21/21 -> 19/21 for mask widening) and re-confirms `sgimtewidth' as the smallest live implementation defect it could establish independently: a one-byte MTE store of 0x5a takes the two-word branch, writes 0x0000005a to the selected word and ZEROES the following one.  *** AND I TRIED TO CHECK THAT CLAIM TWICE AND BOTH DERIVERS WERE MIS-KEYED, which is this project's own `key on what defines the class, not a correlate' lesson landing on me an hour after I quoted it. ***  First attempt keyed on FILENAMES (`-iname '*null*'`): it returned ZERO for all four rounds INCLUDING `#497', which the seat says HAS a preserved receipt -- so the search was measuring whether anyone had put the word in a filename.  Second attempt keyed on a directory GUESS derived from the round number: it mapped `#499' to lunaabsentcpu, which is a different round entirely.  **A null-medium receipt is defined by what it CONTAINS -- a run against a stale copy or a comment-only file scoring zero -- not by its name or its directory**, and neither of my searches tested that.  So the claim is neither confirmed nor refuted here: it stays OWED, and the check owes a key derived from the artifact's content and a round-to-directory map read from the CHANGELOG rather than guessed."
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:24369:     "note": "DESIGN PASS.  **ALL FOUR PREMISES HOLD** -- it independently stripped comments and strings and confirmed the corrected census exactly: 12 statements + 4 disabled + 3 prose mentions = 19.  *It said so plainly rather than inventing a defect, which is the `briefforcedwrong` correction working on the first brief written after it.*  **But operationally only ELEVEN are reachable: the twelfth is UNREACHABLE CODE** (`if (len == 4)` / `else if (len != 4)` are exhaustive, so the trailing `else` never runs) -- filed as sgimtewidth, together with the live defect that dead guard was hiding.  **THE FOUR DISABLED EXITS ARE NOT A SOUND BLANKET PRECEDENT** and it took each one separately: the getputpixel default assigns `*color = random()` (filed sgirandompixel); the tileptr check safely suppresses a put but leaves a stale colour on a get; the XFER addr_src arm is in draw_primitive() and logs-and-continues while the stride registers do not govern the traversal; the dst_y_step arm logs and continues while the value never controls stepping.  Dispositions: BACKED ACCEPT for the six `TODO: read from CRIME_RE_` arms (missing READ paths, existing arrays); IMPLEMENT or DECLINE+LATCHED ANNOUNCE for the five do_mte_transfer exits, returning BEFORE transfer writes -- and it noted `src > MTE_TLB_C` today rejects FILLS as well as copies.  `horrible_getputpixel()` IS per-pixel but **none of the twelve uncommented exits is inside it**, so command-wide validation belongs before the loops.  FAULT confirmed mechanically available on this IP32/MIPS path.  Answer preserved at regress/artifacts/sgirekills/codex_design_20260906.txt"
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:24473:     "note": "PRETABLE.out (13 KB) written 19:00, BEFORE table 1 -- the mandated order proven by mtime rather than asserted.  All four checks present, and the GUARD-FIELD one discharged N/A WITH ITS REASON, which is the rule about blanks obeyed.  SIBLING PARITY earned its place immediately: the six arms are NOT symmetric -- for TLB_A/B/C and TEX the width guard protects correctness only, for LINEAR_A/B it is additionally the only thing preventing an out-of-bounds array write -- so the fix ships DIFFERENT comments at the two groups instead of papering over the asymmetry.  It also refuted the brief's memory-safety phrasing by compiling offsetof, named sgidewidth as instructed, and turned up the sgi_mte dead arm, which I folded into sgimtewidth rather than filing as the new row it proposed.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:24475:     "outcome_note": "[UPHELD-AND-CORRECTED-MY-BRIEF]  CHECKED, not accepted.  Its PRETABLE refused the brief's 'load-bearing HOST memory safety' phrase and measured `offsetof` instead; I re-derived the consequence and it holds -- the over-run is intra-struct, so ASAN cannot see it and the phrase would have shipped an L4 in PRODUCT CODE.  Its sibling-parity finding (the six arms are NOT symmetric) is verified against the source.  Its sgi_mte dead-arm finding was CORRECT but DUPLICATE -- already filed as sgimtewidth from a pass-1 reading -- so it was folded, not filed; I verified the negation by opening the file first.  Seventeen of its filed predictions then matched measurement exactly, including d_returnzero's 35/35 escape which it predicted IN WRITING before running.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:24663:   "note": "*** A GUEST WRITING THE TEXTURE ARRAY SEQUENTIALLY CORRUPTS ENTRIES IT HAS ALREADY WRITTEN, AND 81 OF 112 ENTRIES CANNOT BE REACHED AT ALL.  Found by the codex seat under the STANDING LICENSE while answering a scope question about a different defect in the same file. ***  **THE EVIDENCE IS THE SIBLING COMPARISON, and it is as strong as this kind of finding gets: three arms of the same handler do it one way and the fourth does it another.**  `CRIME_RE_TLB_A`, `_TLB_B` and `_TLB_C` each compute their index as  `int tlbi = ((relative_addr & 0x1ff) >> 1) & 0xff;`  -- a BYTE offset shifted right by 1 to give a HALFWORD index.  `CRIME_RE_TEX` computes  `int tlbi = ((relative_addr & 0xff) >> 3) & 0xff;`  on an array of the same kind, `uint16_t re_tex[112]`.  **THE ARITHMETIC, and it is decidable from the declaration alone.**  The window is `relative_addr >= CRIME_RE_TEX && < CRIME_RE_TEX + 0xe0` -- 224 bytes -- and 112 halfwords is exactly 224 bytes, so window and array correspond one-to-one.  The write loop steps `tlbi++` once per halfword, so an 8-byte `sd` writes FOUR entries.  But `>> 3` advances the STARTING index by only ONE per 8-byte step:      offset 0x00 -> tlbi 0, writes 0,1,2,3      offset 0x08 -> tlbi 1, writes 1,2,3,4   <- OVERLAPS THREE      offset 0x10 -> tlbi 2, writes 2,3,4,5   <- OVERLAPS THREE  So consecutive aligned writes overwrite three quarters of their predecessor.  And the top of the window, `0xdf >> 3` = 27, reaches index 30 at most: **entries 31..111 are unreachable by any access.**  **`>> 1` gives exactly the right answer at both ends**, which is what makes this a typo rather than a design: `0x00 >> 1` = 0, and `0xdf >> 1` = 111 -- precisely the last valid index.  The three siblings already use it.  *** THIS IS NOT THE WIDTH-KILL CLASS AND MUST NOT BE FOLDED INTO IT. ***  `widthkillclass` is a guest ENDING THE HOST by choosing an access size; this is SILENT DATA CORRUPTION inside a legal, correctly-sized access.  Different class, different fix, different detector -- and the round-scope rule's same-semantic-class arm fails, so it files rather than joining `#497`.  **Arguably worse in one respect and better in another:** it is silent, so nothing announces it and a guest gets wrong texture data rather than a dead emulator; but it corrupts only emulated device state, where a host kill ends the process.  Ranked med on that trade, not high.  *** WHAT A ROUND HERE OWES, and one of them is a trap: ***  a rung-3 witness (the machine and window are already established -- `-E sgi -e ip32`, `dev_sgi_re_init(machine, mem, 0x15000000)`, so TEX is at guest `0xb5001600`); and **an ADJACENT-OFFSET oracle, because a naive 'the sd changed something' accept row STAYS GREEN under this defect** -- the write does land, just in the wrong cells and on top of its neighbour.  The row has to write two adjacent 8-byte groups and read back the FIRST, which is the only shape that sees an overlap.  SOURCE-DERIVED, NOT MEASURED.  The seat said so explicitly and this row repeats it: no probe has been run against TEX.  The arithmetic is checkable from the declaration and was re-verified here against the three sibling arms, but the behaviour has not been observed on a running guest.  *** THE COUPLING TO `sgirekills` IS ENFORCEABLE BY ONE ROW, SO THIS IS NOT A HARD PREREQUISITE.  Adjudicated 2026-08-30, recorded in both rows. ***  If `sgirekills` ships TEX's read arm MIRRORED -- using the same `>> 3` expression as the store -- then a ONE-SIDED fix of this row reds its round-trip row loudly: store `>> 1` against read `>> 3` writes cells 4-7 and reads 1-4, and the reverse likewise, while the JOINT fix keeps identity and the row stays green.  **So this row's fix becomes a DECLARED TWO-SITE EDIT with a tripwire already in place**, rather than a change that could silently half-land.  **AND THE ORDER IS GENUINELY FREE, with a real trade either way**: this row first makes TEX's ACCEPT identical in kind to the TLB arms, and its own red-first witness needs no readback at all because the store loop's `debug(\"d->re_tex[%i] = ...\")` lines already show the index.  `sgirekills` first is licensed by severity -- host kills outrank silent corruption -- and hands this row a strictly stronger witness afterwards, since a write-write-read overlap can then be observed through real readback.  *** RE-DERIVED BY ENUMERATION 2026-08-30, after a sibling row in this family was WITHDRAWN as a false out-of-bounds. ***  All three of this row's figures were walked rather than reasoned: every `relative_addr` in `0x1600-0x16df` mapped through `((ra & 0xff) >> 3) & 0xff` with four writes per access.  **Maximum index 30 -- so this row is NOT an OOB and does not claim to be**; `re_tex[112]` has ample headroom.  **81 cells unreachable** and **3 entries of overlap** between consecutive 8-byte writes, both matching the filed claim exactly.  *The withdrawn sibling was filed source-derived and never range-checked; this one now has been.*  *** CONFIRMED BY ENUMERATION AND RE-DIAGNOSED: IT IS A UNIT MISMATCH, NOT A WRONG SHIFT. ***  The codex seat derived the TEX geometry from the CRIME spec -- 28 entries of 64 bits at 0x1600 + 8n -- and my walk of the shipped code found base indices running exactly 0..27.  **Independent agreement on 28, one from the specification and one from the source.**  28 x 8 = 224 = 0xe0 (the window bound) and 28 x 4 = 112 (the array), so `re_tex` is 28 entries of FOUR HALFWORDS each.  **`>> 3` therefore selects the ENTRY correctly.  The array is indexed in HALFWORDS, and entry n begins at halfword 4n -- so the code writes [n],[n+1],[n+2],[n+3] where it must write [4n]..[4n+3].**  Verified over all 28 entry starts that `(addr & 0xff) >> 1` is identically `4 * ((addr & 0xff) >> 3)`, so the three siblings' `>> 1` IS the correct expression.  **NOT A MEMORY-SAFETY DEFECT** -- max index 30 against a [112] array.  Model correctness only: the guest's texture data lands in the wrong cells and clobbers its own previous write.  *The fix comment matters more than the edit here: `>> 1 because re_tex is halfword-indexed and each 64-bit entry spans four halfwords` survives a future reader, while `>> 1 to match the siblings` invites someone to \"fix\" the siblings instead.*  *** THE NATURAL OBJECTION TO THE PROPOSED `>> 3` -> `>> 1` FIX IS THAT IT COULD RUN OFF THE END OF A [112] ARRAY.  CHECKED 2026-08-31: IT CANNOT, AND THE MARGIN IS EXACTLY ZERO. ***  Recorded because an unanswered overflow objection is what stalls a one-token fix at review, and because \"exactly zero margin\" is a fact the fixing round must know rather than rediscover.  With `>> 1` the index is the halfword offset: window `[0x00, 0xe0)`, so `tlbi` spans 0..111 and the array is `uint16_t re_tex[112]` -- one-to-one, as the sibling comparison predicts.  The write loop steps `tlbi++` per halfword, so an 8-byte `sd` writes FOUR entries.  **The worst case is the last legal 8-byte slot.**  8-byte accesses are 8-ALIGNED (the guest path cannot deliver an unaligned `len == 8` -- ordinary `ld`/`sd` raise ADEL/ADES without calling `memory_rw`, and the `ldl/ldr/sdl/sdr` helper masks the address itself), so the reachable offsets are `0x00, 0x08, ... 0xd8`.  At `0xd8`: `tlbi = 0xd8 >> 1 = 108`, writing **108, 109, 110, 111** -- the last four entries, and `0xd8 + 8 = 0xe0` is exactly the window bound.  **Fits with nothing to spare.**  *The zero margin is why this is worth writing down rather than waving through: `0xdc` would give `tlbi = 110` and write 110..113, two entries past the array -- so the fix's safety rests ENTIRELY on the 8-byte alignment invariant, exactly as the LINEAR arms' own `#497` comment says theirs does.*  A round shipping this owes the same alignment note the LINEAR arms already carry, and its detector owes an accept row at the LAST slot (`0xd8`) -- which is the window's far bound, and per the 2026-08-31 correction to the accept-row rule, a SECOND row or an ANCHORED oracle for the near bound.  *** PASS-1 PACKET-FED PANEL, 2026-08-31 (`_scratchpad/panel_20260831_045142`): THE CORE CLAIM IS UPHELD BY THREE INDEPENDENT DERIVATIONS, AND THE ZERO MARGIN IS CONFIRMED TWICE. ***  Reachability: **31 of 112 entries, 81 unreachable** -- glm and deepseek both derived 31 independently and it matches this row's original figure; minimax wrote 30/82 and is off by one (`[0,30]` is 31 entries).  **glm named a premise the brief never stated and I had assumed**: the whole `& 0xff` analysis needs `CRIME_RE_TEX` to be 256-aligned.  Checked: `0x1600`, so it holds, and every sibling window's mask matches its own base as well.  **minimax supplied a better warrant than the one I led with**: `112 x 2 = 224 = 0xe0` is an INTRINSIC identity between array size and window size, where the sibling comparison is merely comparative and can be answered with *maybe TEX is different on purpose*.  **The fixing round should lead with the size identity.**  **deepseek answered the detector-design question**: no index-only mutant survives a detector that writes and reads back EVERY halfword, so full distinctness closes the clause-(f) hole here.  **MY BRIEF CARRIED A COUNT DEFECT, caught by two seats independently**: it said *four* windows compute an index and then inlined *six*.  Immaterial to the arithmetic and it is precisely the class the brief's own opener warns about -- recorded rather than quietly fixed.  *** STILL UNSETTLED, AND THE SEATS WERE RIGHT NOT TO SETTLE IT: whether hardware might legitimately space texture registers every 8 bytes.  There is no datasheet for this device in `_scratchpad/`.  That question decides FIX vs DOCUMENT and is the one thing a file-reading seat or a manual could still change.***  *** THE PANEL'S ONE OPEN QUESTION IS NOW CLOSED WITHOUT A DATASHEET, AND THE DISPOSITION IS FIX RATHER THAN DOCUMENT.  Derived 2026-08-31. ***  All three seats correctly declined to settle *whether hardware might legitimately space texture registers every 8 bytes*, and `ls _scratchpad/*.pdf` confirms there is **no SGI, CRIME, IP32 or O2 document** among the fifteen present.  **But the question turns out not to gate the fix, because BOTH candidate readings give the SAME index expression.**  *(a)* the struct's own declaration -- `uint16_t re_tex[112]`, indexed by halfword -> `offset >> 1`.  *(b)* the rival reading the missing spec would license -- **28 x 64-bit entries, one per 8-byte store**.  The write loop emits FOUR halfwords per store, so entry `k` must START at halfword `4k`, i.e. the correct index is `(offset >> 3) * 4`.  **For 8-ALIGNED offsets `(offset >> 3) * 4` IS `offset >> 1`** -- checked across the window (0x00->0, 0x08->4, 0x10->8, 0x40->32, 0xd0->104, 0xd8->108, identical both ways).  *** SO `>> 1` IS CORRECT UNDER BOTH HYPOTHESES AND `>> 3` IS WRONG UNDER BOTH -- under (b) it is short by exactly the factor of 4 that the loop consumes. ***  **This also strengthens minimax's intrinsic-identity warrant rather than replacing it.**  That argument (`112 x 2 = 224 = 0xe0`) depends on the `uint16_t` CHOICE being right, and the header does not confirm it: `crmfbreg.h` carries an explicit `/* 32bit entries ... */` comment for **LINEAR_A/B and not for TEX**, so the element size there is GXemul's own reading.  *The both-hypotheses derivation is the stronger form precisely because it does not need the element size to be settled at all.*  **And the window bound is confirmed from the header rather than from the guard**: the next register is `CRIME_RE_CLIP_IDS = 0x16e0`, so `[0x1600, 0x16e0)` really is 224 bytes and the guard's `+ 0xe0` is not an invention.  *What is still genuinely unknown, and is now a DOCUMENTATION question rather than a blocker: how many entries the real device has and how wide they are.  The fix is right either way; the array's declared type is a separate, smaller question that no evidence in this tree settles.*  *** THE `sgirekills` / `sgitexindex` COUPLING, ESTABLISHED 2026-08-31 AND RECORDED ON BOTH ROWS SO NEITHER CAN BE CLOSED IN IGNORANCE OF THE OTHER. ***  **The fact, which is settled:** `sgitexindex`'s `>> 1` is correct under BOTH candidate readings of a TEX entry -- 112 halfwords indexed `offset >> 1`, or 28 64-bit entries where entry `k` starts at halfword `4k`, since `(offset >> 3) * 4 == offset >> 1` for 8-aligned offsets.  **So the store's shift is wrong INDEPENDENTLY of the missing datasheet**, and there is no SGI document in `_scratchpad/`.  **Consequence for `sgirekills`:** its TEX read arm can only ACCEPT in the MIRRORED form -- reusing the store's `>> 3` -- because only that round-trips what the store wrote.  Mirroring therefore enshrines a KNOWN-WRONG index in a second place, and gives it a green round-trip row.  *When someone later corrects the shift, that row goes RED because of their correct fix.*  **Consequence for `sgitexindex`, in the other direction:** the TEX read arm is TODAY a live host kill, so **no guest can read back what those writes stored** and the aliasing is currently unobservable from inside the guest.  Fixing `sgirekills` is what would expose it.  That bears on severity and on ORDER, and neither row had recorded it.  *Neither consequence decides the disposition -- that is the question `sgirekills` is held for and a panel is answering it.  What is recorded here is the COUPLING, which is a fact either way.*\n\n*** MAIN-LOOP MEASUREMENT 2026-08-31 (not a seat answer; recorded here rather than in `entries` so it cannot be counted toward the panel). ***  evidence: grep -rn re_tex src/ (3 occurrences, all dev_sgi_re.c)\n\nMEASURED BY GREP, 2026-08-31, settling hold_question (1) and reframing (3): `re_tex' occurs EXACTLY THREE TIMES in the whole tree, all in dev_sgi_re.c -- the declaration, ONE write site, and a debug() on the very next line echoing what was just stored.  Nothing else in src/ mentions it.  *** SO THE `>> 3' ARM IS THE SOLE WRITER (question 1: YES, the fix's assumption holds and changing it repairs rather than splits the mapping) AND THE ARRAY HAS NO CONSUMER ANYWHERE IN THE MODEL. ***  That is stronger than the row's own framing, which says only that no GUEST can read it back because the READ arm is fatal+exit: even with that arm de-fatalised there is nothing else that consumes the array, so the aliasing's only channel today is the debug() text.  Consequence: it lowers this row (no consumer, no observable corruption) and couples it to `sgirekills', whose mirrored-TEX-read fix would create the array's FIRST consumer.  Severity change and ordering NOT applied unilaterally -- referred to the adjudicator, and hold_question (2) referred to a non-author seat because it asks for exactly that.  A NEW sub-question was raised by the grep and sent with it: the both-hypotheses derivation needs `relative_addr' 8-ALIGNED, and the arm's guard is `len != 8' which does NOT imply alignment -- the same premise that made an earlier census wrong by 4x.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:24721:     "ev": "dev_sgi_re.c re_tex[112] then re_linear_a[32]; debugger_cmds.c put d = printf not guard; sgi_re_width_probe.py stores at base+8 greps re_tex[1]; sgi_re_unmapped_probe.py 0x6d8 greps re_tex[27]",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:24722:     "outcome_note": "*** TWO SEATS, TWO FINDINGS, AND THEY DISAGREED ON A CHECKABLE FACT.  Settled by opening the files; the adjudicator was right and the reading seat was wrong. ***\n\nFINDING 1 (reading seat, VERIFIED): the proposed >> 1 fix would INTRODUCE a new OOB write.\n  >> 3 is bounds-safe UNCONDITIONALLY -- at every in-window offset it yields indices 27..30, always inside re_tex[112].  >> 1 is bounds-safe ONLY UNDER 8-ALIGNMENT.  Computed: at offset 0xdf (or 0xde) the loop writes halfword indices 111, 112, 113, 114 -- and the struct declares uint16_t re_tex[112] immediately followed by uint32_t re_linear_a[32], so 112..114 land IN re_linear_a.  Silent, in-struct, ASAN-invisible: the sgilinearoob class, newly created by a hardening fix.\n  AND THE UNALIGNED ROUTE EXISTS.  MIPS guest paths enforce alignment before memory_rw.  The DEBUGGER does not: put d prints WARNING: address isn't aligned -- a printf, NOT a return -- and proceeds to store_64bit_word, whose memory_rw carries CACHE_DATA with no NO_EXCEPTIONS, so a DM_DEFAULT handler IS invoked.  Verified by reading the arm.\n  DESIGN CHANGE ADOPTED: make the precondition LOCAL rather than inherited from another file --\n      if (len != 8 || (relative_addr & 7))     /* the existing SGIRE_W_TEX_LEN latch */\n  Under the addition licence, trading an unconditionally-safe index for a conditionally-safe one on the strength of an invariant argued in a DIFFERENT FILE and known to have an exception is not a licensed trade.\n\nFINDING 2 (adjudicator, VERIFIED, and it CORRECTS finding 1's companion claim): the reading seat said the committed detector is structurally blind because every TEX row sits at in-window offset 0 where every shift maps to 0.  **THAT IS FALSE.  TWO COMMITTED ROWS ALREADY PIN THE >> 3 DECODE:**\n    regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py   -- its accept row stores at base + 8 and greps re_tex[1]; a correct >> 1 decode gives re_tex[4]\n    regress/sgi_re_unmapped_probe.py -- its last-slot row uses in-window 0xd8 and greps re_tex[27]; a correct >> 1 decode gives re_tex[108]\n  The seat appears to have read the width probe's TABLE ENTRY (which lists the base address) without following to the store, which is at base + 8.  Settled by opening both files.\n  CONSEQUENCE, and it is an obligation rather than a note: **fixing the shift REDS BOTH ROWS**, so the oracle-correction rule governs -- re-pin FIRST, measure the committed build RED under the new expectations (re_tex[1] -> [4], re_tex[27] -> [108]), THEN change the code.  Enumerate that re-pin set in PRETABLE.out BEFORE table 1, re-pinned deliberately rather than discovered red.\n\nA STRONGER WARRANT that needs neither a datasheet nor the alignment premise, and should lead the row: the loop advances tlbi++ PER HALFWORD, so tlbi IS a halfword index, and the halfword index of a byte offset is offset >> 1.  >> 3 makes the start expression advance one per 8 bytes while the body advances one per 2 -- DIFFERENT UNITS.  Decidable from four lines, at any offset.\n\nTWO DEFECTS IN THE EXISTING WARRANT.  (1) The row's end-point argument is demonstrated at offset 0xdf -- an UNALIGNED offset where the fix is WRONG; the correct end point is the last 8-aligned offset 0xd8 -> 108..111.  Conclusion survives, warrant does not.  (2) sgilinearoob states the premise in the FALSE form: len == 8 forces 8-byte alignment.  It forces nothing.\n\nSEVERITY: MED -> LOW, and the downgrade is CONDITIONED, not free.  The MED warrant sentence -- the guest gets wrong texture data -- is refuted: re_tex is the only one of six register arrays in the file with no reader, and it is not a reader-not-yet-written but storage for a function the model does not implement (horrible_getputpixel has no TEX case; the MTE's MTE_TLB_TEX falls to default).  **RE-PROMOTE the day any consumer is proposed** -- a sgirekills TEX read-back, real RE texture rendering, any new dump channel -- and the fix must land BEFORE that consumer.\n\n*** ORDERING: THE SEVERITY ORDER INVERTS.  This LOW row runs FIRST, as a prerequisite of the HIGH one. ***  LOW is the label; first is the schedule; the two are severed deliberately, because ranked LOW and left in severity order it would sit behind the round that depends on it.  Cost is about zero calendar time -- sgirekills still owes its own rung-3 witness and that work parallelises with this small shift round.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:24723:     "note": "*** TWO SEATS, TWO FINDINGS, AND THEY DISAGREED ON A CHECKABLE FACT.  Settled by opening the files; the adjudicator was right and the reading seat was wrong. ***\n\nFINDING 1 (reading seat, VERIFIED): the proposed >> 1 fix would INTRODUCE a new OOB write.\n  >> 3 is bounds-safe UNCONDITIONALLY -- at every in-window offset it yields indices 27..30, always inside re_tex[112].  >> 1 is bounds-safe ONLY UNDER 8-ALIGNMENT.  Computed: at offset 0xdf (or 0xde) the loop writes halfword indices 111, 112, 113, 114 -- and the struct declares uint16_t re_tex[112] immediately followed by uint32_t re_linear_a[32], so 112..114 land IN re_linear_a.  Silent, in-struct, ASAN-invisible: the sgilinearoob class, newly created by a hardening fix.\n  AND THE UNALIGNED ROUTE EXISTS.  MIPS guest paths enforce alignment before memory_rw.  The DEBUGGER does not: put d prints WARNING: address isn't aligned -- a printf, NOT a return -- and proceeds to store_64bit_word, whose memory_rw carries CACHE_DATA with no NO_EXCEPTIONS, so a DM_DEFAULT handler IS invoked.  Verified by reading the arm.\n  DESIGN CHANGE ADOPTED: make the precondition LOCAL rather than inherited from another file --\n      if (len != 8 || (relative_addr & 7))     /* the existing SGIRE_W_TEX_LEN latch */\n  Under the addition licence, trading an unconditionally-safe index for a conditionally-safe one on the strength of an invariant argued in a DIFFERENT FILE and known to have an exception is not a licensed trade.\n\nFINDING 2 (adjudicator, VERIFIED, and it CORRECTS finding 1's companion claim): the reading seat said the committed detector is structurally blind because every TEX row sits at in-window offset 0 where every shift maps to 0.  **THAT IS FALSE.  TWO COMMITTED ROWS ALREADY PIN THE >> 3 DECODE:**\n    regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py   -- its accept row stores at base + 8 and greps re_tex[1]; a correct >> 1 decode gives re_tex[4]\n    regress/sgi_re_unmapped_probe.py -- its last-slot row uses in-window 0xd8 and greps re_tex[27]; a correct >> 1 decode gives re_tex[108]\n  The seat appears to have read the width probe's TABLE ENTRY (which lists the base address) without following to the store, which is at base + 8.  Settled by opening both files.\n  CONSEQUENCE, and it is an obligation rather than a note: **fixing the shift REDS BOTH ROWS**, so the oracle-correction rule governs -- re-pin FIRST, measure the committed build RED under the new expectations (re_tex[1] -> [4], re_tex[27] -> [108]), THEN change the code.  Enumerate that re-pin set in PRETABLE.out BEFORE table 1, re-pinned deliberately rather than discovered red.\n\nA STRONGER WARRANT that needs neither a datasheet nor the alignment premise, and should lead the row: the loop advances tlbi++ PER HALFWORD, so tlbi IS a halfword index, and the halfword index of a byte offset is offset >> 1.  >> 3 makes the start expression advance one per 8 bytes while the body advances one per 2 -- DIFFERENT UNITS.  Decidable from four lines, at any offset.\n\nTWO DEFECTS IN THE EXISTING WARRANT.  (1) The row's end-point argument is demonstrated at offset 0xdf -- an UNALIGNED offset where the fix is WRONG; the correct end point is the last 8-aligned offset 0xd8 -> 108..111.  Conclusion survives, warrant does not.  (2) sgilinearoob states the premise in the FALSE form: len == 8 forces 8-byte alignment.  It forces nothing.\n\nSEVERITY: MED -> LOW, and the downgrade is CONDITIONED, not free.  The MED warrant sentence -- the guest gets wrong texture data -- is refuted: re_tex is the only one of six register arrays in the file with no reader, and it is not a reader-not-yet-written but storage for a function the model does not implement (horrible_getputpixel has no TEX case; the MTE's MTE_TLB_TEX falls to default).  **RE-PROMOTE the day any consumer is proposed** -- a sgirekills TEX read-back, real RE texture rendering, any new dump channel -- and the fix must land BEFORE that consumer.\n\n*** ORDERING: THE SEVERITY ORDER INVERTS.  This LOW row runs FIRST, as a prerequisite of the HIGH one. ***  LOW is the label; first is the schedule; the two are severed deliberately, because ranked LOW and left in severity order it would sit behind the round that depends on it.  Cost is about zero calendar time -- sgirekills still owes its own rung-3 witness and that work parallelises with this small shift round."
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:24782:     "outcome_note": "guard is the right shape; the ADDITION LICENCE is not satisfied.  Q3 is a floor finding about the REPAIR: delete the alignment half of the guard and an anchored detector row at aligned offset +0x08 STILL PASSES, because that row is aligned -- clause (d) shaped, guard kept and its repair removed.  So a position-A row set owes an UNALIGNED row.  Q2: the licence is NOT satisfied -- re_tex has no reader, observable severity is nil, the only warrant offered is future correctness, and that is not a measured delta.  Would not ship absent a transferred warrant.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:24783:     "note": "guard is the right shape; the ADDITION LICENCE is not satisfied.  Q3 is a floor finding about the REPAIR: delete the alignment half of the guard and an anchored detector row at aligned offset +0x08 STILL PASSES, because that row is aligned -- clause (d) shaped, guard kept and its repair removed.  So a position-A row set owes an UNALIGNED row.  Q2: the licence is NOT satisfied -- re_tex has no reader, observable severity is nil, the only warrant offered is future correctness, and that is not a measured delta.  Would not ship absent a transferred warrant."
