tools/pipeline/ledger.json:30423:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07.  **FOUND BY THE CODEX SEAT** on the `sgirebufuninit' pass-1 panel, under the standing\n*\"if you found something ELSE, say so\"* licence -- it is not what that brief asked about.\n\n**VERIFIED BY ARITHMETIC BEFORE FILING.**  The two arms disagree about which byte holds which channel:\n\n    PUT  case DE_MODE_TYPE_RGB:   buf[0] = *color >> 24;  buf[1] = *color >> 16;\n                                  buf[2] = *color >> 8;   buf[3] = 0;\n    GET  case DE_MODE_TYPE_RGB:   *color = (buf[1] << 24) + (buf[2] << 16) + (buf[3] << 8);\n\nPUT stores the top three bytes at indices **0,1,2**; GET recombines from **1,2,3**.  Storing `0x11223300'\ngives bytes `11 22 33 00', which GET reads back as **`0x22330000'**.  A round trip through the model's own\nRGB mode does not return what was written.\n\n*** THE REASON THIS MATTERS BEYOND THE BUG: EVERY ANALYSIS THIS WEEK, MINE INCLUDED, CALLED THE FOUR\nIMPLEMENTED COLOUR ARMS `CORRECT' AND TREATED ONLY THE `default:' ARMS AS SUSPECT. ***  The\n`sgirebufuninit' brief says so explicitly -- *\"NO colour arm writes buf[4..7] -- not one of the five,\nincluding the four CORRECT ones\"*.  codex's words: *\"Calling all implemented color arms 'correct' is\nunwarranted.\"*  It is right, and the phrase had been repeated unchecked across three documents.\n\nWHICH SIDE IS WRONG IS **NOT** ESTABLISHED HERE and must not be guessed: RGBA uses 0,1,2,3 symmetrically on\nboth sides, and ABGR uses 0..3 reversed on both sides, so RGB is the only asymmetric pair -- but whether the\nhardware packs RGB at the low three bytes or the high three is a DATASHEET question this row does not\nanswer.  A round taking it owes that reference, or a comparison against a real framebuffer dump.\n\nNO WITNESS.  Reachability is not in doubt (RGB is documented and implemented) but nothing here shows a guest\ndriver round-tripping through it.\n\n*** WHICH SIDE IS WRONG IS NOW SETTLED, AND NO DATASHEET WAS NEEDED.  THE ANSWER WAS ONE FILE AWAY, IN THE\nCONSUMER OF THESE VERY BYTES.  Derived 2026-09-07, same day as the filing. ***\n\nThis row said the packing question needed *\"a datasheet reference or a comparison against a real framebuffer\ndump\"*.  **It needed neither.**  `dev_sgi_gbe.c`'s tick reads the SAME tile memory these arms write and turns\nit into host framebuffer pixels:\n\n    color = (buf[i]<<24) + (buf[i+1]<<16) + (buf[i+2]<<8) + buf[i+3];\n\nSo the in-tree contract is unambiguous: **byte index 0 is the MOST significant channel.**  Against that:\n\n    PUT  RGB    buf[0]=c>>24  buf[1]=c>>16  buf[2]=c>>8   buf[3]=0        AGREES with the consumer\n    PUT  RGBA   buf[0]=c>>24  buf[1]=c>>16  buf[2]=c>>8   buf[3]=c        AGREES\n    GET  RGBA   (buf[0]<<24)+(buf[1]<<16)+(buf[2]<<8)+buf[3]              AGREES\n    GET  ABGR   (buf[3]<<24)+(buf[2]<<16)+(buf[1]<<8)+buf[0]              AGREES (deliberately reversed)\n    GET  RGB    (buf[1]<<24)+(buf[2]<<16)+(buf[3]<<8)                     *** DISAGREES -- off by one ***\n\n**THE DEFECT IS IN THE `GET' ARM, AND IT IS THE ONLY ARM IN EITHER SWITCH THAT DISAGREES WITH THE CONSUMER.**\nIts sibling `GET RGBA' does the identical job one index lower and is correct, which is about as strong an\ninternal warrant as this tree offers.  The fix is the index triple: `(buf[0]<<24)+(buf[1]<<16)+(buf[2]<<8)'.\n\n**WHY THIS MATTERS FOR THE ROUND'S SHAPE:** it converts an open design question into a one-line fix with a\nnamed oracle, and it supplies the detector's channel for free -- a PUT-then-GET round trip through RGB must\nreturn what was written, which is a value oracle, not a survival one.\n\n*The general lesson, and it is the option-set rule wearing another hat: I reached for an EXTERNAL authority\nbefore asking whether the tree already answered the question.  `ls _scratchpad/*.pdf' confirms there is no SGI\nor CRIME document here at all -- only MIPS manuals -- so the external route was not merely slower, it was\nCLOSED, and the in-tree consumer was the only available oracle the whole time.*",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:30427:   "id": "sgimtedepth4dead",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:30431:   "title": "dev_sgi_re.c do_mte_transfer(): `if (depth == 4)' is UNREACHABLE -- depth is 8 << (0..3), so it is only ever 8, 16, 32 or 64",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:30433:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07.  **FOUND BY THE CODEX SEAT** on an unrelated brief, and VERIFIED at HEAD before filing.\n\n    int depth = 8 << ((mode & MTE_MODE_DEPTH_MASK) >> MTE_DEPTH_SHIFT);\n\n`MTE_MODE_DEPTH_MASK' is `0x00000300' and `MTE_DEPTH_SHIFT' is 8, so the extracted field is TWO BITS, 0..3,\nand `depth' takes exactly the values **8, 16, 32, 64**.  The function then tests `if (depth == 4)', which is\ntherefore **never true** -- the guarded block is dead code.\n\ncodex's reading of what is lost: the dead block is *\"its intended RGBA construction\"*, so *\"its helper calls\nretain CI\"* -- i.e. the transfer runs in colour-index mode where the author meant RGBA.  **That second half\nis NOT verified here** and is the part a round would have to establish; what IS verified is that the branch\ncannot execute.\n\n*** IT ALSO REFUTES A PREMISE OF THE `sgirebufuninit' BRIEF, which is why it was filed rather than noted. ***\nThat brief asks whether callers constrain the (colour type, depth) pair.  Here is a caller that TRIES to and\nFAILS SILENTLY -- so the model's own producer does not supply the pair its author intended.  Any round\nreasoning about what callers guarantee must account for this one.\n\nLOW because a dead branch cannot itself corrupt anything; the SEVERITY lives in whatever the missing RGBA\nconstruction was supposed to do, which is unestablished.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:30443:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07.  **RAISED BY THE CODEX SEAT** on the `sgirebufuninit' pass-1 panel, as a narrowing of one of\nmy own SAFE verdicts, and VERIFIED at HEAD before filing.\n\n`DEVICE_TICK(sgi_gbe)' reads one tile line into a stack buffer and then converts it to host framebuffer\npixels.  **The read length and the conversion bound disagree:**\n\n    int len = tilex < width_in_tiles ? 512 : (partial_pixels * bytes_per_pixel);\n    if (len > 512) len = 512;                                  /* OB-14 */\n    cpu->memory_rw(cpu, cpu->mem, base + 512 * line, buf, len, MEM_READ, ...);\n    ...\n    for (int i = 0; i < 512; i += bytes_per_pixel) {           /* <-- 512, NOT len */\n            ... color built from buf[i] .. buf[i+3] ...\n            get_rgb(d, color, &fb_buf[fb_i], ...);\n    }\n\n`memory_rw' fills exactly `len' bytes.  The loop reads `buf[0..511]' **unconditionally**.  `len < 512' is\nreachable: on the last tile column (`tilex >= width_in_tiles') it is `partial_pixels * bytes_per_pixel'.  So\n`buf[len..511]' is read UNINITIALISED and fed through `get_rgb()'.\n\n*** SCOPE, STATED HONESTLY AND MATCHING THE SEAT'S OWN HEDGE: THIS IS NOT A DISCLOSURE. ***  Only\n`fb_len = (len / bytes_per_pixel) * 3' bytes are forwarded by `dev_fb_access', and `fb_len' is derived from\n`len' -- so the pixels built from the uninitialised tail are COMPUTED AND DISCARDED.  codex said exactly this:\nthe smaller forwarded prefix *\"prevents me from calling this a demonstrated guest disclosure; it nevertheless\ninvalidates the blanket initialization-safety claim.\"*  That is the right call and it is why this is LOW.\n\n**What it IS: an uninitialised read of up to 511 bytes per tile line** -- undefined behaviour, and the kind of\nthing an ASAN/MSan sweep is supposed to catch.  `build-asan/' exists and is kept clean, so a cheap first step\nis to check whether a sweep already flags this and, if not, why not.\n\n**The fix is one token: `i < len' instead of `i < 512'.**  Note the neighbouring OB-14 clamp shows someone has\nalready reasoned about this loop's bounds ONCE -- and bounded the READ while leaving the CONSUMPTION at a\nliteral.  That is the same shape as [[sgirebufuninit]] one file over: a previous round fixed the SIZE and did\nnot ask what fills, or here consumes, the difference.\n\nNO WITNESS.  Reachability of `len < 512' is established by reading; nothing here shows a guest driving it.",

